# React Development Rules

These rules apply only to React components and hooks.

---

# Components

- Use functional components only.
- Use TypeScript for every component.
- One component per file.
- One default export per page component.
- Shared UI components should use named exports.
- Keep components under 300 lines whenever possible.
- Split large components into smaller reusable components.

---

# Component Structure

Use this order inside every component:

1. Imports
2. Types / Interfaces
3. Constants
4. Hooks (useState, useMemo, useRef, etc.)
5. useEffect
6. Event handlers
7. Helper functions
8. JSX
9. Export

---

# Props

- Always define an interface for component props.
- Use descriptive prop names.
- Avoid passing unnecessary props.
- Prefer explicit props over generic objects.
- Destructure props in component parameters.

Example:

```tsx
interface PlayerCardProps {
  player: Player;
  onClick: () => void;
}

export function PlayerCard({
  player,
  onClick,
}: PlayerCardProps) {
```

---

# State

- Keep state as local as possible.
- Don't duplicate state.
- Don't store derived values in state.
- Prefer computed values with useMemo when necessary.
- Group related state logically.

Good

```tsx
const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
```

Avoid

```tsx
const [players, setPlayers] = useState([]);
const [playersCount, setPlayersCount] = useState(players.length);
```

---

# useEffect

Use useEffect only for:

- API requests
- subscriptions
- timers
- synchronization

Never use useEffect for calculations.

Always clean up effects when necessary.

Example

```tsx
// ---------- Fetch Players ----------
useEffect(() => {
    fetchPlayers();
}, [page]);
```

---

# Event Handlers

Use descriptive names.

Good

```tsx
handleSave()
handleDelete()
handleUpload()
handlePageChange()
```

Avoid

```tsx
save()
click()
run()
```

---

# JSX

Keep JSX clean.

Prefer

```tsx
<PlayerCard />

<PlayerCard />

<PlayerCard />
```

Instead of

```tsx
<PlayerCard /><PlayerCard /><PlayerCard />
```

Avoid deeply nested JSX.

Extract reusable sections into components.

---

# Conditional Rendering

Prefer early return.

Good

```tsx
if (loading) {
    return <Loading />;
}
```

Instead of

```tsx
return (
    <>
        {!loading && ...}
    </>
)
```

---

# Lists

Always use stable keys.

Good

```tsx
key={player.id}
```

Avoid

```tsx
key={index}
```

unless there is no alternative.

---

# API Requests

Keep fetch logic inside hooks or dedicated functions.

Example

```tsx
async function fetchPlayers() {
    ...
}
```

Not directly inside JSX.

---

# Forms

- Use controlled inputs.
- Keep validation separate.
- Use meaningful data-testid values.
- Keep form handlers together.

---

# Styling

- Tailwind CSS only.
- No inline styles.
- Follow TennisConnect design language.
- Mobile-first.
- Keep className readable.
- Group related utility classes together.

Example

```tsx
className="
    flex
    items-center
    justify-between
    rounded-xl
    border
    p-4
"
```

---

# Comments

Add comments only where useful.

Good

```tsx
// ---------- Fetch Players ----------

// ---------- Save Profile ----------

// ---------- Upload Avatar ----------
```

Avoid

```tsx
// Button

// Div

// useState
```

---

# Performance

- Avoid unnecessary re-renders.
- Memoize expensive calculations only.
- Avoid premature optimization.
- Reuse components whenever possible.

---

# File Organization

Large pages should be split.

Example

pages/

PlayerProfile.tsx

components/

PlayerHero.tsx

PlayerInfo.tsx

PlayerStats.tsx

PlayerGallery.tsx

PlayerActions.tsx

---

# Accessibility

Always include:

- alt for images
- aria-label where needed
- semantic HTML
- keyboard accessibility

---

# AI Instructions

When generating React code:

- Match existing TennisConnect style.
- Preserve current layout.
- Do not rewrite working components.
- Reuse existing components first.
- Keep code readable.
- Explain complex logic with short comments.
- Prefer consistency over introducing new patterns.
